    <h1>Q-Point (Quiescent Point) in Transistor Circuits</h1>

    <p>
        The <strong>quiescent point (Q-point)</strong> is the steady-state DC operating
        point of a transistor circuit when no AC input signal is applied. It sets
        the baseline DC voltage and current levels to ensure the transistor operates
        in the correct region for amplification.
    </p>

    <h2>Key Differences Between BJT and MOSFET Q-Points</h2>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>BJT Q-Point</th>
            <th>MOSFET Q-Point</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Control Parameter</td>
            <td>Base Current (IB)</td>
            <td>Gate-to-Source Voltage (VGS)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Key DC Currents</td>
            <td>Collector Current (IC)</td>
            <td>Drain Current (ID)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Key DC Voltages</td>
            <td>Collector-Emitter Voltage (VCE)</td>
            <td>Drain-to-Source Voltage (VDS)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Target Linear Zone</td>
            <td>Active Region</td>
            <td>Saturation Region</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>BJT Q-Point Parameters</h2>

    <p>
        For a BJT to amplify an AC signal without distortion, the Q-point must be
        established in the <strong>Active Region</strong>.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Variables:</strong> (VCE, IC)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="formula">
        IC = β × IB
    </div>

    <div class="formula">
        VCE = VCC − (IC × RC)
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Target Placement:</strong> Ideally, VCE = VCC / 2 for maximum
            symmetrical signal swing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Risk:</strong> Beta (β) changes with temperature, causing Q-point
            drift and possible thermal runaway.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>MOSFET Q-Point Parameters</h2>

    <p>
        For a MOSFET amplifier, the Q-point must be established in the
        <strong>Saturation Region</strong>.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Variables:</strong> (VDS, ID)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="formula">
        ID = ½ μn Cox (W/L) (VGS − Vth)²
    </div>

    <div class="formula">
        VDS = VDD − (ID × RD)
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Condition for Saturation:</strong>
            VDS > (VGS − Vth)
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Advantage:</strong>
            Gate current IG ≈ 0, making biasing simpler than BJT circuits.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why Q-Point Biasing Matters</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Prevents Distortion:</strong>
            Keeps the signal from clipping at cutoff or saturation.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Controls Power Consumption:</strong>
            Determines the idle power dissipated by the circuit.
        </li>
    </ul>

